  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This 2013 review by Barry King has begun to register scholarly uptake, with one documented citation and one recorded downstream reuse. The available evidence points to a modest but real afterlife in later research, while the broader attention record remains thin and early.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Barry King\u2019s review of Chris Rojek\u2019s <em>Fame attack: the inflation of celebrity and its consequences</em> sits within the scholarly conversation on celebrity, media, and culture, and it has so far accumulated a small but clear trace in later literature. OpenAlex records one citation, and the reuse graph shows one downstream article reusing the work, indicating that the piece has been taken up in subsequent academic writing rather than disappearing without trace.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citing record is narrow and highly concentrated. The single citing article appeared in 2024 in <em>PHILANTHROPY Journal of Psychology</em> and concerns celebrity worship and eudaimonic well-being among ARMY fandom members in Indonesia. That context suggests the review\u2019s influence is being carried forward through related discussions of celebrity attachment and fandom, even if the current evidence base is limited to one documented citation.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The surrounding impact signals are also sparse. Scite shows one mentioning paper, but no supporting or contradicting counts are recorded, and no public event-data mentions are available from the archived source. This pattern is consistent with an early, lightly documented citation profile rather than a broad public or media footprint.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">There is, however, some indication of institutional and pedagogical reach. WorldCat holdings show that the work is held in library collections, which supports discoverability for teaching and research use. OpenAIRE also records a repository instance for the article, suggesting additional scholarly accessibility. Overall, the record shows a piece with limited but genuine academic uptake, chiefly within later work on celebrity worship and fandom.</p>",
